Case:     T43849 "IPv4_ARP - Local Proxy ARP: Functionality". This case HAS authored
          step text: ck.db AWP-4357 (internal_id 25060), a unique exact-title match,
          517 characters, quoted verbatim:
            "Configure DUT and BackupSW / Connect both SW via VLAN 23 / Configure DUT
             as follows: interface VLAN23, iP address 23.1.1.1/24, ip local-proxy-arp /
             Configure BackupSW as follows: interface VLAN23, ip address 23.1.1.2/16 /
             From BackupSW, ping 23.1.1.3 / Expect ping to fail, but with ARP using DUT
             MAC address. DUT responds to ARPs on an interface for other IP hosts that
             would be on the same interface. This is a "one armed router" situation eg
             when private vlans are configured. DUT MAC address will be saved on
             BackupSW ARP table"
          The authored expected result: the ping FAILS, and BackupSW's ARP table holds
          the DUT's MAC for 23.1.1.3. The deliberate /24-vs-/16 mask mismatch is the
          mechanism -- with 23.1.1.2/16, BackupSW believes 23.1.1.3 is on-link and ARPs
          for it instead of routing to it. The authored steps are followed as written on
          real hardware, with swi_b as BackupSW, and are SUPPLEMENTED -- not replaced --
          by crafted-frame legs that measure the ARP exchange itself, plus a no-route
          control, a mask-boundary control, removal and reboot persistence. Also graded
          against X960 platform support TPS row 24 "Local Proxy-ARP" (priority 1, "Yes",
          note column empty) and the IP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide, Local
          Proxy ARP. 5 steps as executed, plus a baseline.
