  And the discriminator that makes the ping meaningful: the ping fails in BOTH states,
  so "the ping failed" proves nothing by itself. What changed is WHO reports the
  failure.
    feature OFF (STEP 0b): "From 23.1.1.2" -- BackupSW itself, unable to resolve. The
                           packets never left BackupSW.
    feature ON  (here):    "From 23.1.1.1" -- the DUT. BackupSW resolved 23.1.1.3 to the
                           DUT's MAC and FORWARDED the echo requests to it; the DUT
                           accepted them, tried to resolve the real 23.1.1.3 on vlan23
                           (the 3 broadcast requests from 23.1.1.1 in the capture),
                           found nothing, and returned the unreachable itself.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the authored expected result is met in full on real hardware -- the
     ping fails, BackupSW's ARP for 23.1.1.3 is answered by the DUT, and the DUT's MAC
     is in BackupSW's table. The capture and the unreachable's source address show the
     traffic genuinely reached the DUT, so the PASS rests on the mechanism and not on
     identical ping text that appears in both states.

STEP 3 -- the mask boundary (supplementary; no authored text for this limb)  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the authored /24-vs-/16 mismatch makes BackupSW treat all of 23.0.0.0/16 as
  on-link. Does the DUT local-proxy-answer for an address inside BackupSW's /16 but
  OUTSIDE its own /24? Both targets in the SAME leg.

  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L2-mask-boundary --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --arp 23.2.2.2,23.1.1.3 --sender-ip 23.1.1.5 \
